00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:42.440
up and running.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.430
MATTHEW TAMSETT:
Hey, I'm Matthew,

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.780
I'm the lead data
scientist here at Qubit.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.490
I've been here for
about three years.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.380
Before that I was an
experimental particle

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:51.100
physicist.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.810
And I worked at CERN.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.350
And since leaving academia,
I've been working Qubit Festival

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.690
here as a data scientist
and more recently,

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.990
as a lead, which means I lead a
small team of about three data

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:05.650
scientists who use statistics,
machine learning, and tools

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.140
like this to try and create
a better online experience

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.773
for our customers' customers.

00:03:09.773 --> 00:03:10.690
AJA HAMMERLY: Awesome.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.050
GABI FERRARA: So you said,
Matt, that you were a physicist.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.210
And now you work
as data science.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:19.240
How it was like to work
as a physicist turning

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.130
to data scientist now?

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:23.350
MATTHEW TAMSETT: It really
is surprisingly common.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.710
So when I first graduated
after finishing my PhD,

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:29.070
there was this
strange uncertainty

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.820
that opened up in front
of me and in front

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:32.380
of lots of my colleagues,
where we didn't really

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.220
know what to do.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.340
You could carry on in physics,
or you could get another job.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.665
But no one was really
clear what that meant.

00:03:38.665 --> 00:03:40.290
But over the next
four years, I started

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.730
to hear this term data science
coming up more and more.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.290
And at first, I didn't
really know what it meant.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:48.070
And then after sort of
investigating it a bit more,

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:48.880
we saw--

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.730
me and my colleagues realized
that there was this whole world

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.940
out there emerging to do with
the proliferation of data

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.460
and high-performance computing
and things like this.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.910
That meant there was
a whole new area where

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:03.400
we could apply very
similar skills to those

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.730
we'd used in our academic
lives but in industry.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:09.010
So at fist, the whole
idea of leaving academia

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.130
was a bit scary.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:12.262
So I think when I
learned more about it,

00:04:12.262 --> 00:04:13.720
I started to realize
it actually it

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.959
was quite similar to that
everyday life in academia.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:18.640
You still had the
same sort of problems

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.709
where you had like
huge data sets,

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:24.130
and you had-- it wasn't really
clear what the noisy signals

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.790
in them meant.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.400
And there was now
this place where

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.190
I could work and apply those
same skills but in industry.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:34.810
GABI FERRARA: So what does Qubit
does with that data science

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:36.815
and as a company?

00:04:36.815 --> 00:04:38.440
MATTHEW TAMSETT: We
do a lot of things.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.573
I think what you could say
is we sort of split what

00:04:40.573 --> 00:04:42.490
we're doing to sort of
a few different realms.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.530
The first one is just
simply measurement.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.440
So Qubit's history is
rooted in A/B testing,

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.068
where you had multiple
variants of a single website.

00:04:51.068 --> 00:04:53.110
And what you wanted to
know is which one of those

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.890
works best for my user
base at large or maybe

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:59.290
for individual groups
of users within that.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:01.510
And measuring the difference
between those two things

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.770
is not necessarily
an easy problem.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:05.425
The reason it's not
necessarily easy

00:05:05.425 --> 00:05:07.300
is because you don't
really care about things

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.520
like click-through rates
or conversion rates.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.980
You really want to care about
things like revenue and loyalty

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:13.855
and things like this.

00:05:13.855 --> 00:05:16.390
And these can be quite
complicated noisy

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:17.650
distributions.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.060
So one of the
things my team does

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.000
is work on modeling these
revenue distributions

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.610
and looking for
small differences

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:28.400
that you can measure by changing
the website in various ways.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.420
On top of that, we also try
and predict things about users.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.750
So Qubit has access to
what we collect, like

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:37.270
a vast store of
behavioral information

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.700
to do with how individual
businesses interact

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.110
with the website.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:42.790
And one great thing
you can do with that

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:45.640
is use an individual's
historic data

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:48.860
to change the online experience
and to personalize this,

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:49.360
we'd say.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.040
So if you look at a
lot of hats, then we

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.820
want to show you the
most relevant hats.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.450
ML and data sites
gives you a way

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:58.490
of doing that not only in
the rearwards-looking way,

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:01.030
but also in a more
forward-looking predictive way.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:04.030
So my team spends a reasonable
time building models

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.670
to try and predict interesting
facets of these uses

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.250
that we can then use to
personalize the experience,

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.190
whether this is from
recommendation models, which

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.440
products we think you're
going to need to engage with,

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.170
whether you're going to churn,
what your next spend will be,

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:18.700
things like that.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.890
Then in addition, we
also do a lot of work

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:25.060
around dividing our customers'
customers again up into sets

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.910
or clusters of people that
behave in similar ways

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:30.190
partially to allow our
clients to understand

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.620
who is interacting with
their website and why.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:35.142
But also so that we can build
these more specific models off

00:06:35.142 --> 00:06:36.600
of them that take
into account, you

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.235
know, different
facets of the users.

00:06:38.235 --> 00:06:39.610
GABI FERRARA: I
know your product

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.797
works because I'm a customer
for one of your customers.

00:06:42.797 --> 00:06:43.880
MATTHEW TAMSETT: Oh, yeah?

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.630
GABI FERRARA: And I do receive
recommendations all the time.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:50.470
And I ended up
buying everything.

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:53.030
Between giving this
value to your customers,

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.720
do you think the
impact is also bigger,

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.350
I mean on revenue, of course?

00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:01.240
But like, there's this merchant
that because I bought a baby

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.070
bag, they think I
have a baby now.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.750
And they keep sending
me baby stuff.

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.410
But I want to say to
them, I don't have a baby.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:08.952
[BABY CRYING]

00:07:08.952 --> 00:07:09.868
MATTHEW TAMSETT: Yeah.

00:07:09.868 --> 00:07:12.010
GABI FERRARA: So do you
think the aggregates help

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:12.850
people shop?

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:15.267
MATTHEW TAMSETT: I think at
its most basic level, it does.

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:17.200
So like the simplest
recommendation algorithms

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:19.060
you can come up
with just use facets

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.122
of the population at
whole to try and work out

00:07:21.122 --> 00:07:22.330
what you're going to do next.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:24.370
In that case, the
most common action

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:25.780
of people who
bought baby bags is

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:28.250
to buy other
baby-related things.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:31.250
I think the challenge is
taking that sort of global view

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:33.610
and making it more nuanced
to you as a consumer,

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:37.360
either by building algorithms
that are clever enough

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.150
to realize that, OK, we've shown
you these baby outfits five

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:42.430
or six times, and you're
not engaging with them.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.410
Maybe we should show
you something different.

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:49.010
Or by maybe giving you a greater
aspect of influence over that.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:51.542
So if you could just
tell the algorithm

00:07:51.542 --> 00:07:53.500
you are interested in
that, that would probably

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.510
be the quickest,
simplest way for it

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:57.888
to be able to learn that.

00:07:57.888 --> 00:07:59.680
So, yeah, and that's
incredibly challenging

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.400
to try and use this to give each
individual the best experience

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:05.530
when individuals vary so much.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:07.030
I think one of
the big challenges

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:09.080
here is that we're
dealing with people.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:12.100
And people are all
different and complicated.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.500
And even compared to,
again, in my previous life

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.950
as a physicist, the underlying
systems were complicated.

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:20.416
But there were only
finitely complicated.

00:08:20.416 --> 00:08:22.666
Whereas a person can have
so many different variations

00:08:22.666 --> 00:08:25.345
and act in different
ways day to day.

00:08:25.345 --> 00:08:27.130
So trying to come
up with things that

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:30.880
use the limited amount of data
that you have cleverly but can

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.179
also count for this really high
amount of individual variation

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:35.840
is a huge challenge.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.600
AJA HAMMERLY: So
this is super cool.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.023
I'm super into
personalization-based stuff

00:08:40.023 --> 00:08:41.440
and spend a lot
of my early career

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:43.630
working on products with
personalization built in.

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:46.838
But I'm actually super curious
about how y'all are using GCP.

00:08:46.838 --> 00:08:48.880
Because we have you on
the podcast because you're

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.190
customers.

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:51.760
So what does your
stock look like?

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.147
How did you make decisions?

00:08:53.147 --> 00:08:55.480
What tools work really well
for these giant data science

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:56.450
problems?

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:58.390
RAVI UPRETI: As Matt
mentioned before,

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.260
we collect vast behavioral data.

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:04.000
So in order to do that,
how we use GCP is basically

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.580
we have a gateway where
all these events come

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:08.360
through the Qubit platform.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:11.320
And we use actually
quite a bit of GCP

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:14.080
from Pub/Sub, which would
be responsible for acting

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.390
these buffers between
different data flows.

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:19.330
And data flows are
these logics, which,

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:22.720
for example, business logics,
like validations or enriching

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:24.820
things to actually
making sure that we

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.100
let our data enter
into BigQuery that we

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:28.660
use as a data warehouse.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.110
On top of that, of course, once
we have the data in BigQuery,

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.180
then we let other
systems, for example,

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.480
Matt's machine learning systems,
to actually use that and create

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.370
something, in a
smarter intelligence,

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:40.600
thinks on top of that.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.780
Also what we do with
these GCP pipelines are

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.850
it enables us to be real
time, so we can have

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:47.290
real-time solutions as well.

00:09:47.290 --> 00:09:51.010
For example as a client or as
a user is browsing the site,

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:53.290
they might be changing from
a segment to a segment.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:55.510
All of a sudden, a
first-time customer

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:58.040
becomes a first-time buyer
and things like that.

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.980
So we can out these real-time
solutions working as well.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.970
And that helps us deliver better
personalizations, more reactive

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:05.617
or more real-time
personalizations.

00:10:05.617 --> 00:10:08.200
AJA HAMMERLY: So what kinds of
decisions did you have to make?

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:09.700
And what did the
process look like

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:11.700
before you brought in
GCP's tools, like Pub/Sub,

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:13.690
and I think you all are
BigQuery users, too?

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:14.440
RAVI UPRETI: Yeah.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:14.830
Yeah.

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:16.705
AJA HAMMERLY: And what
does it look like now?

00:10:16.705 --> 00:10:19.690
Because the ability to do
machine learning in real time

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:20.532
is kind of awesome.

00:10:20.532 --> 00:10:22.240
MATTHEW TAMSETT: So
yeah, for the past it

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:23.740
was quite different, actually.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:25.810
Before we used to manage
our infrastructure

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:28.360
ourselves for asking
