00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:31.420
from these providers to
actually having our own Hadoop

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:32.310
clusters over there.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:34.060
Something that flows
[INAUDIBLE] real time

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.340
right now would before
look like, OK, we

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:38.260
see a behavioral information.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:40.750
We store it somewhere
in a storage bucket.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:42.940
And then we'd have
these massive batch jobs

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:45.580
that probably run once
a day in the evening

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:47.620
when we have to kind of
manually, most likely,

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:50.203
provision, so that, oh, we have
enough machines so that we can

00:10:50.203 --> 00:10:53.500
run these jobs on time, to
actually an analyst waiting

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.610
for the next day to see, OK,
how was my day yesterday.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:58.972
And how did our customers
behave yesterday?

00:10:58.972 --> 00:11:00.430
They had to [? wait ?]
[? today. ?]

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:02.450
This actually moving
on to BigQuery

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:04.720
as an example, changed
from, well, you

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:06.080
could just query it faster.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:07.960
So before, when we had
the Hadoop clusters,

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:10.660
you could run these hive
queries, which sometimes

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:12.580
even used to take
like hours to do

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:15.850
simple things like attribution
or other [INAUDIBLE],,

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:18.580
queries, to actually now being
able to do them sometimes

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:19.390
in seconds.

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:22.090
[INAUDIBLE] was in
minutes was amazing.

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:24.280
And, yeah, decision
for us-- so we already

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:26.510
had the pattern, the
pattern that works for us.

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:29.470
And it was so easy for us to
map these individual components

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:31.480
into something
that GCP provides.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:33.190
So for example, if
we're using Kafka,

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:35.080
we know, OK, we could
just use Pub/Sub.

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:36.970
If we had Apache
Storm pipeline, we

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:39.970
knew we could just change it
very easy to Cloud Dataflow.

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:42.460
In terms of warehouse,
yeah, we're using Redshift.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:45.110
And then we knew, OK, we
can use BigQuery for that.

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:47.680
We did try to sort of
Franken-cloud stuff, where

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.560
we had a few processing
happening on GCP

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.477
and trying to actually land data
to Redshift, which we realized

00:11:53.477 --> 00:11:55.810
quite quickly was not working
for us because we were not

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:58.810
really able to stream data
that fast into Redshift

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:00.310
as we were into BigQuery.

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:01.750
And that was again, a big win.

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:04.000
AJA HAMMERLY: So it sounds
like you're using BigQuery.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.417
It sounds like you're using
BigQuery partly because of how

00:12:06.417 --> 00:12:07.930
fast it can ingest data.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:10.075
Are you y'all using
BigQuery at ML at all?

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:12.760
MATTHEW TAMSETT: Yeah, so
we've been using BigQuery ML

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:14.440
I think slightly over a year.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:16.810
I think we got involved
in the [INAUDIBLE] of it

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:17.940
fairly early on.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:19.938
So some of our initial
reactions were, oh,

00:12:19.938 --> 00:12:22.230
this is only like logistic
and linear regression, which

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:23.710
were quite simple models.

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:25.510
So at first, we
found it a little bit

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:28.660
easy to say, OK, that's not
for us because we were really

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:31.340
interested in getting the
absolute best performance

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:32.505
from any individual model.

00:12:32.505 --> 00:12:33.880
But over time, I
think we sort of

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.413
realized that the real power of
that system wasn't in the fact

00:12:37.413 --> 00:12:38.830
that it would train
the best model

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:40.640
for every single scenario.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:45.100
It was that it would enable us
to do it really, really fast.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:47.920
Qubit's infrastructure is
based around streaming events

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.740
from online touch points,
like browsers in real time,

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:52.990
and adjusting this
into BigQuery.

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:55.930
Now, because that's where all
of our behavioral data ends up,

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:58.840
we already have a lot of tools
that make this actionable

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:00.400
to you in your website.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:02.620
So by that I mean
you can query out

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:04.780
what a user's done in
the past and enable

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:07.910
that to adapt their
future website experience.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:09.710
Now, when BigQuery
ML came along,

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.470
that meant that we
could do not only

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:13.930
sort of historic
backwards-looking queries,

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:16.380
we could also train
new models to predict

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:17.470
forwards-looking things.

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:20.260
And that was done in the
system that was already

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:21.730
commoditized within Qubit.

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:25.780
So by that I mean we already
had all of the tooling and data

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:26.980
engineering around it.

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:29.380
That meant data
scientists' job changed

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:31.570
from writing a model
in the notebook,

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.310
building that into
a Python module,

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:36.460
deploying that somewhere,
worrying about how it scales

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:39.730
and how it's scheduled, and
the performance and the API,

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:42.160
to writing one query.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.410
And the performance of that
model, like I said before,

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:46.285
is never going to be
better than the sort of,

00:13:46.285 --> 00:13:47.610
the custom hand-built pipeline.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.110
But it meant that
instead of taking weeks

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:53.590
maybe to build out a new use
case, you could do it in a day.

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:56.110
So I think once we
realized the speed

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:58.960
with which that
allowed us to innovate,

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:00.270
it completely changed things.

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:03.388
And we really decided to
invest a lot of time into it.

00:14:03.388 --> 00:14:05.680
And one of the reasons I
think that works well at Qubit

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:07.030
is because we have this--

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:11.560
there's so many things you can
do with a user behavioral data.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:15.160
And to explore all of
them in minute detail

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.390
would take many lifetimes.

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:20.470
So instead what we want to
do is explore a lot of them

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:22.870
fairly rapidly and work
out which ones give us

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:24.520
the biggest bang for our buck.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:27.220
It's very easy to sort of judge
the quality of your models

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.260
by an offline performance
metric, you know,

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:31.570
how well it does
in your test set.

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:33.450
But really, for us,
what we care about

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:36.280
is does it change a
user's end experience?

00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:39.757
Does this make a shop, an
online shop, more compelling

00:14:39.757 --> 00:14:41.590
and give them something
that makes them want

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:42.982
to come back again and again?

00:14:42.982 --> 00:14:44.440
So for us, the
proof of the pudding

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:48.280
is not whether this thing gets
a really good [INAUDIBLE] score

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.940
or whatever or some
other offline metric.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:53.340
It's about whether when
deployed online drives

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.540
a meaningful change
to revenue or loyalty

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:57.530
or some other measure
of engagement.

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:00.010
So what we want to do there is
to explore all this rapidly,

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.740
get it in front of users, and
be able to learn from that.

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:04.510
And with the system
we already had

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:06.670
and the fact that BigQuery
ML is essentially just

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:09.193
BigQuery, yeah, we really
dove into it head first.

00:15:09.193 --> 00:15:11.860
We've churned out more models, I
think, in the past three months

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:14.210
using this than we did in
the previous couple of years.

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:16.220
And it's really
interesting to see.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:17.920
And then like I said
again, these things

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:19.900
end up in the hands of
our customers' customers

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:20.727
very quickly.

00:15:20.727 --> 00:15:22.060
GABI FERRARA: I have a question.

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:25.990
So do you think that the
ability of using BigQuery ML--

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:28.240
because in the
end, it's basically

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:30.520
SQL that you're
writing on BigQuery ML.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:33.460
Do you think that's a lower
barrier for, like let's say

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:35.920
you have new data scientists.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:38.890
They may be able
to predict models

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:41.080
in a bit more of
cost benefit way,

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:43.630
as you said it before,
because the models,

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:45.970
now it doesn't take
that long to be refined.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:47.590
Do you think that helps anyhow?

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:49.330
MATTHEW TAMSETT:
I'd say yes and no.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:51.550
So most data scientists
when they come in

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:53.938
are very fluent in Python
or R or something like that.

00:15:53.938 --> 00:15:56.230
So they tend to be very good
at doing their own feature

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:58.190
transformations and
churning their own model.

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:01.332
SQL is almost like a second
language to a lot of people

00:16:01.332 --> 00:16:02.290
certainly we have here.

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:05.650
And though they're good at
it, it doesn't necessarily

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:07.090
give them all the
tools they'd be

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:08.660
used to in their everyday life.

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:11.540
So in some ways,
it's not as fast.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:13.540
However, the breadth
of the people

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:16.300
within the company you
have access to SQL skills

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:19.280
is much larger than those
you have Python skills.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:22.300
So what it does do is enable
people in other departments

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:23.870
to get involved a
lot more easily.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:27.910
So product managers or product
specialists or analysts

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:29.680
or client engineers,
all of these people

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:32.000
suddenly can join in, as well.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:35.470
I also think with SQL there's
a pretty steep learning curve.

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:38.500
But once you're over
the hill, then it

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:40.987
does become quite
quick to iterate.

00:16:40.987 --> 00:16:42.820
So I'd say that's a bit
mixed, in some ways.

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:45.980
I think you've got a much wider
pool of talent you can draw on.

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:48.910
But sometimes it's not
the sort of power tools

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:50.710
that data scientists
would be used to.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.810
GABI FERRARA: And
it's good to do

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:55.960
one thing like a [INAUDIBLE],,
take this out of the way

00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:59.130
and do a quick thing instead
of having to create a network

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:00.610
and work all over.

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:05.140
Did you know or do you use
somehow other data sources,

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:08.575
other GCP products, other
databases, anything like that?

00:17:08.575 --> 00:17:10.450
MATTHEW TAMSETT: So I
suppose Ravi would also

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:11.283
have a view on this.

00:17:11.283 --> 00:17:12.930
But we use them for
different purposes.

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:15.550
So almost all of our behavioral
data ends up in BigQuery.

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:17.905
But we'll use different
databases for other things.

00:17:17.905 --> 00:17:20.619
So for instance, my team
uses Cloud Data Store

00:17:20.619 --> 00:17:24.910
to store configurations and
model summaries and things

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:26.040
like this.

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:28.450
And I'm not entirely clear
on the reasons for that.

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:29.200
Ravi can say more.

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:31.960
But it enables us to have
our Kubernetes deployments

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:33.070
dependent upon them.

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:35.440
So that if I just have a
model that I train in batch,

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:38.170
I can update the [INAUDIBLE] in
Cloud Data Store and restart.

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:40.150
That-- yeah, I guess
it's horses for courses.

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:41.920
Perhaps you should talk
about this a bit more, Ravi.

00:17:41.920 --> 00:17:42.670
RAVI UPRETI: Yeah.

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:45.910
Apart from BigQuery, yes,
we use Bigtable a lot.

00:17:45.910 --> 00:17:49.103
We use Memorystore
Redis as well a lot.

00:17:49.103 --> 00:17:51.520
And this is something we are
experimenting with more right

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:51.790
now.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:53.170
We have recommendations
right now

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:55.270
that uses [INAUDIBLE]
Redis in some other cloud.

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:57.100
And we want to actually
try it out here.

00:17:57.100 --> 00:17:59.590
I think as Matt said
before, we usually

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:01.810
used to get access to
these things like as they

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:04.420
are introduced, like
in maybe alpha version.

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:06.280
So we were one of
the first customers

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:09.640
to actually try Cloud Bigtable,
where we build our solution

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:12.040
stash, which is where
you can keep certain user

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:15.310
attributes temporarily and then
access them online and kind

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:18.700
of add a bit of personalization
to certain experiences.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:21.030
And we use Cloud SQL as well.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:24.077
Yeah, so these are the
databases that we use.

00:18:24.077 --> 00:18:26.410
GABI FERRARA: Just a follow
up, you mentioned Cloud SQL.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:28.900
And do you use the new
feature for federated queries

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:31.000
inside BigQuery to Cloud SQL?

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:32.942
It's a beta feature.

00:18:32.942 --> 00:18:34.400
RAVI UPRETI: No,
we don't use that.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.090
But that is something, yeah,
we would love to try actually.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:38.830
Because right now
we have a process

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:40.300
where we have
certain information,

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:43.510
but information, let's say,
around segments or experiences

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.910
that we could keep
in MySQL boxes, which

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:48.910
we would like to be used in
[INAUDIBLE] queries as well.

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:51.220
Right now what we do is we
try to sync it to BigQuery.

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:53.230
We do a bit of transformation
and sync it there.

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:55.750
So using the federated one
would be a good thing for us

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.010
to experiment actually.

00:18:57.010 --> 00:18:59.110
What we have used
before is having

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:00.210
a bit of external tables.

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:02.398
So for example, if the
data is stored on GCS,

00:19:02.398 --> 00:19:03.940
we have those kind
of external tables

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:05.878
and try to use it in
our different queries.

00:19:05.878 --> 00:19:07.420
GABI FERRARA: Tell
me-- you mentioned

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:11.800
Python and R. What else eats
on your stock language wise?

00:19:12.463 --> 00:19:14.630
MATTHEW TAMSETT: And so for
my team's point of view,

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:17.380
we have a bit of
history reflective

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:19.420
of the skills of
people who've joined

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:20.820
the company over the years.

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:23.990
So we have things in R, in
sort of the middle of our Stats

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:25.960
Engine, which is
the tool that we

00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:28.860
use for measuring these
differences between A/B tests.

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:30.370
There's a bit of Stan in there.

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:34.390
And then layered on top of
that, we have C++ and Python.

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:36.400
And then from the data
science point of view,

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:37.780
that's pretty much
where it ends.

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:41.645
And we're in the process of
unifying that as we go along.

00:19:41.645 --> 00:19:43.270
I think part of the
problem that we had

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:45.640
is that people come in
with different skill sets.

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:47.493
They're not software
engineers by training,

00:19:47.493 --> 00:19:49.660
so they want to use the
tools that they've got close

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:51.550
at hand to solve problems.

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:54.160
But that can lead
to a proliferation

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:55.790
of languages over time.

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:59.800
So we're trying to sort of unify
under one umbrella in my team,

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:00.617
and that's Python.

00:20:00.617 --> 00:20:02.200
All over the place
we used loads more.

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:03.730
So Ravi can talk about that.

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:05.510
RAVI UPRETI: Yeah,
for us we, use

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:08.500
Apache Beam SDK for
the data flows, which

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:10.390
is the Java one that we use.

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:13.360
We have lots of web services
that are built with a mix

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:15.790
of being in [? Go ?]
[? line ?] or in Java.

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:18.230
And then we do use
Python as well,

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:19.880
which is mostly for
our recommendations

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:21.760
ecosystem, the services
that we have there.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:25.450
We use Python mostly because we
wanted data scientists as well

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:26.810
to get involved there.

00:20:26.810 --> 00:20:28.450
Rarely, but we
have a few systems

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:29.920
which use [INAUDIBLE] as well.

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:32.000
And yeah, we use Apache Spark.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.600
And we use-- actually
we use Dataproc from GCP

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:38.190
as well, where we spin up these
thousands of clusters where

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:39.910
we run our recommendation jobs.

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:41.920
And actually we use
quite a lot here,

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:43.792
spanning all these
different services.

00:20:43.792 --> 00:20:46.000
AJA HAMMERLY: I know y'all
are doing a lot of events.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:47.542
I got a little blurb
on your company,

00:20:47.542 --> 00:20:49.630
and you're doing tons and
tons and tons of events

00:20:49.630 --> 00:20:51.530
leading to tons and
tons and tons of data.

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:54.700
Do you have any particular
stories, [INAUDIBLE] stories,

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.620
or particularly
interesting anecdotes

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:59.560
you can share with us, days
that have been particularly

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:00.690
good or particularly bad?

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:02.170
I know that lots
and lots of times

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:04.083
we get a bunch of
nerdy folks together

00:21:04.083 --> 00:21:05.500
that like to tell
about those days

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:08.035
where everything went wrong
or everything went great.

00:21:08.035 --> 00:21:09.660
RAVI UPRETI: We used
to have such days,

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:11.950
so, for example, things
like Black Fridays,

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:15.100
where everybody all of a
sudden is looking for deals.

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:17.050
And the traffic
spikes are going up.

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:18.720
And the days when
we were managing

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:21.340
our own infrastructures,
those were the days

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:23.770
when it reminds you
of these movies where

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:24.970
you're putting out a fire.

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:27.430
So every day it would be like,
OK, people entering office

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:30.270
at 11:00 AM or 12:00,
and you're sort

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:32.270
of standing there clapping
for them because they

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.510
were up all night actually
managing these things.

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:36.110
So it used to be that bad.

00:21:36.110 --> 00:21:38.140
But moving to GCP, we actually--

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:41.980
Black Fridays kind of became
a bit more manageable for us.

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:44.050
We could auto scale very easily.

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:46.510
We could provision systems
whenever we wanted to do,

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:48.760
rather than actually putting
a request in like a month

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:49.848
back or weeks back.

00:21:49.848 --> 00:21:51.640
So those are the days
where I joined Qubit.

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:53.880
I remember everyday,
oh, this is down.

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:56.990
And being new, we didn't get
to get involved too much there.

00:21:56.990 --> 00:21:59.380
But I knew other
people actually really,

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:00.830
really being involved there.

00:22:00.830 --> 00:22:02.320
So those were the tricky days.

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:04.120
Christmas could
get tricky as well.

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:06.160
GABI FERRARA: So GCP
gave you back your sleep?

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:07.243
RAVI UPRETI: Yeah, it did.

00:22:07.243 --> 00:22:10.040
AJA HAMMERLY: I can't think of
a more resounding endorsement

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:11.230
than that.

00:22:11.230 --> 00:22:12.660
I totally get that.

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:15.380
Is there anything else that
y'all have experienced from

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:18.230
moving to the cloud versus
being on your own hardware?

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:19.850
Is there something
that makes easier,

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:21.522
stuff that it makes harder?

00:22:21.522 --> 00:22:22.730
RAVI UPRETI: I think, easier.

00:22:22.730 --> 00:22:24.890
Even the journey, the
beginning of the journey

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:29.270
to actually moving to cloud or
to GCP was how easy sometimes

00:22:29.270 --> 00:22:30.703
it can be to prototype stuff.

00:22:30.703 --> 00:22:32.120
So for example, I
remember when we

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:35.740
had to kind of move one of
these pipelines into GCP.

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:37.460
We're like, OK,
we have data flow.

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:38.930
There's the framework.

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:41.810
Let's see how quickly we can
prove that this will work.

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:44.330
And sometimes it used
to be like a day or two,

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:46.250
and we'll be done with
a proof of concept

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:48.350
that this is going
to work for us.

00:22:48.350 --> 00:22:50.240
And in a month or
two, we would have

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:51.940
something which is
ready for production,

00:22:51.940 --> 00:22:54.740
kind of this ability to actually
provide things very fast,

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:57.770
experiment a lot, and see
what really works for us.

00:22:57.770 --> 00:23:00.530
And yeah, having this
serverless nature, where

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:02.900
all the engineers and
the infrastructure

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:05.045
can focus on solving
the problems that matter

00:23:05.045 --> 00:23:07.670
rather than these problems that
everybody's solving everywhere,

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:11.010
we could just focus on the
problems that matter to us.

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:13.670
Yeah, and the speed of new
features coming in also

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:14.938
helps a lot, right?

00:23:14.938 --> 00:23:17.480
Like, every now and then, you'll
have some new feature in one

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:18.170
of these products.

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:19.310
And you can quickly try it out.

00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:20.410
And, yeah, that works for you.

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:21.980
And auto scaling,
obviously, I keep

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:24.663
on saying auto scaling is pretty
big for most of the products.

00:23:24.663 --> 00:23:26.080
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah,
auto scaling's

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.920
great there because that means
you're not paying for stuff.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.050
You're not using-- how
about the data science side?

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:32.135
Is there anything that
is easier once you're

00:23:32.135 --> 00:23:33.760
in the cloud in the
data science realm?

00:23:34.465 --> 00:23:36.230
MATTHEW TAMSETT: So
I think, for me, it's

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:39.530
all about reducing the
amount of data engineering

00:23:39.530 --> 00:23:40.820
my team has to do.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:42.840
So we're not data engineers.

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:45.030
We're not software
engineers by training.

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:46.140
We can do these things.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:47.900
But we're not really
the right people

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:50.390
to be doing them in
terms of speed and impact

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:51.560
and things like that.

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:55.250
So the thing I get most excited
about Cloud Services are tools

00:23:55.250 --> 00:23:57.590
that can remove
part of that pain,

00:23:57.590 --> 00:24:02.420
whether that's through enabling
me to easily train and host

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:05.300
my models in the cloud, or
if it's something as complete

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:08.608
as an [INAUDIBLE] solution,
where if I've got a data set

00:24:08.608 --> 00:24:10.400
and I want to have a
first pass of a model,

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:13.130
I can just click a button and
pay as much as I want and get

00:24:13.130 --> 00:24:14.960
a state-of-the-art
model out at the end.

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:17.240
So for me, it's
all about reducing

00:24:17.240 --> 00:24:21.680
the reliance on other teams and
enabling us to quickly come up

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:26.510
with and test ideas and build
prototype models and scale

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:27.540
and experiment.

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:30.195
So for instance, we had a
period of time after Christmas,

00:24:30.195 --> 00:24:31.820
I think last year,
where we were trying

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:34.010
to build out some
TensorFlow-based

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:35.150
recommendations models.

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:37.940
And we found we needed
40 machines with GPUs.

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:39.380
And we just have those.

00:24:39.380 --> 00:24:40.970
And it's great, and
that was something

00:24:40.970 --> 00:24:44.690
that was true in my
previous job to some extent,

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:45.930
in physics to some extent.

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:47.940
However, you'd be in a queue.

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:50.450
So the difference
there was that you

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:52.910
had these distributed computing
centers at universities

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:54.110
around the world.

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:55.640
But there was only
so many of them.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:59.600
And so you potentially have
access to huge resources.

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.220
But you had to wait
for everybody else

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:02.095
to be done with them.

00:25:02.095 --> 00:25:03.950
Whereas with the
cloud computing,

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:05.390
there is a limit to the scale.

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:07.030
But practically,
we don't see it.

00:25:07.030 --> 00:25:10.340
So if we need 100 machines
at 10 minutes notice,

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:13.430
it really makes
better use of our time

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:16.370
or frees us up, to some
extent, because you're not

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:18.750
sitting around worrying
about the hardware.

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:21.140
You're not worrying about
debugging these distributed

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:23.380
systems that we don't
really understand.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:26.030
It enables you to focus
the time on the statistics

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:28.400
and the machine learning
that we're here to do.

00:25:28.400 --> 00:25:30.070
AJA HAMMERLY: That
totally makes sense.

00:25:30.070 --> 00:25:32.780
It sounds like for both of you,
the advantages of the cloud

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:34.670
are getting to focus
on the parts that

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:37.280
are unique to your business
and/or you find interesting

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:39.070
and not having to focus
on the other bits.

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:42.258
That's why I like it, too.

00:25:42.258 --> 00:25:43.584
Cool.

00:25:43.584 --> 00:25:46.640
If folks are interested
in y'all product,

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:48.302
where can they learn
more about Qubit?

00:25:48.302 --> 00:25:49.760
MATTHEW TAMSETT:
qubit.com, I think

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:51.268
is probably right place to go.

00:25:51.268 --> 00:25:53.060
RAVI UPRETI: We have
certain blogs as well.

00:25:53.060 --> 00:25:55.290
You could try
looking at the blogs.

00:25:55.290 --> 00:25:57.890
Yeah, we usually are quite
active at Google Cloud

00:25:57.890 --> 00:25:59.160
Next and things like that.

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:01.580
So you'll definitely find
YouTube videos for that, too.

00:26:01.580 --> 00:26:02.580
AJA HAMMERLY: Awesome.

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:03.320
Well, we'll make
sure that there's

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:04.580
a link in the show notes
so that folks don't

00:26:04.580 --> 00:26:06.080
have to type in all the issues.

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:07.580
Click on the link.

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:09.900
Well, thank you for being on
the podcast with us today.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:11.030
It was very educational.

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:13.652
I love hearing stories about
people with cloud migrations.

00:26:13.652 --> 00:26:15.110
GABI FERRARA: And
I love databases.

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:17.920
So it's really
nice to see people

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:19.290
talking that [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:19.790
MATTHEW TAMSETT: OK, great.

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:20.460
Thanks a lot.

00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:21.210
RAVI UPRETI: Cool.

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:21.710
Thank you.

00:26:22.445 --> 00:26:24.490
AJA HAMMERLY: So that was
a great conversation we

00:26:24.490 --> 00:26:25.910
had with the folks from Qubit.

00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:27.410
Now time for a
Question of the Week.

00:26:32.690 --> 00:26:35.320
This week's question is, how do
you deploy a Windows container

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:36.140
on GKE?

00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:37.478
Gabi, do you know?

00:26:37.478 --> 00:26:38.770
GABI FERRARA: I possibly don't.

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:40.480
But I read it on the blog post.

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:41.830
And I think I can do it.

00:26:41.830 --> 00:26:43.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:43.270 --> 00:26:45.970
Basically, you need to have your
container image or container

00:26:45.970 --> 00:26:46.930
registry.

00:26:46.930 --> 00:26:49.210
And then once you have
that, there is a caveat.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:53.380
It's you have to have a Linux
pool with one node at least.

00:26:53.380 --> 00:26:55.840
But then you create
a Windows node pool,

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:57.873
and you can have your
containers deploy there.

00:26:57.873 --> 00:27:00.040
That's the only thing that
I saw that was different.

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:03.170
And you need GKE 114
to be running that.

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:04.700
So that's how you do it.

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:05.870
It's on the blog post.

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:07.930
It's basically the
same YAML files

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:10.790
that you create for
Linux deployments.

00:27:10.790 --> 00:27:11.290
Go there.

00:27:11.290 --> 00:27:14.420
Check it out, so you can see
how to do it from Windows.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:15.640
AJA HAMMERLY: That's awesome.

